{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the `habeas` CLI defines and parses its commands and options with `clap`, and tests cover that behaviour.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `src/main.rs` imports `clap::{ArgGroup, Parser, Subcommand}`. `Cargo.toml` depends on `clap` 4.6.7 with the `derive` feature.\n- `Cli` (`#[derive(Parser)]`, line 10) is marked `{impl}`. `Command`, `MdbookCommand` and `HookCommand` (`#[derive(Subcommand)]`) are also marked `{impl}`.\n- `Command` defines the subcommands `mdbook`, `setup`, `status`, `info`, `author` (with the alias `author-evidence`), the hidden `hook`, and `add-input`. Their options, positional arguments and `ArgGroup` constraints are all declared through clap.\n- `main` calls `Cli::parse()` (marked `{impl}` at line 107). A search of the repository found no manual parsing of `std::env::args`, so clap is the only parser.\n- Clap-specific behaviour such as the usage-error text and exit code 2 is not custom code. It comes from clap.\n- The other `{impl}` comments in the same code (for example setup_local_path, authoring and add_input) belong to those requirements. They do not add unmarked behaviour to this requirement.\n\n**Tests**\n- `tests/cli.rs::clap_defines_commands_options_and_usage_errors`:\n  - `--help` output has the clap form `Usage: habeas <COMMAND>` and lists every public command.\n  - The hidden `hook` command is not listed.\n  - `status --help` shows `--all` and `--verbose`.\n  - The `author-evidence` alias resolves to `author`.\n  - Usage errors exit with code 2 and print a usage message. The cases are an unknown option, a missing required positional argument, and conflicting arguments.\n- `tests/commands.rs::add_input_requires_one_target_and_mode_and_resets_changed_records` is also marked as a test of this requirement. It checks the clap `ArgGroup` constraints on `add-input`: both targets given, both modes given, no target, and no mode. Each case exits with code 2 and prints `Usage: habeas add-input`.\n\nThe tests cover the main points of the design space: help, subcommands, options, alias, hidden command, and failure cases. I found no gaps.",
  "references": "c3c091a5580a2047da77d06cee9d1f88dddd56e8faa5831de3f402b557cd31a4",
